e hits a little  closer to home it's a story about the  tower that fell in Jerusalem Jesus tells  us that 18 people were killed when they  were crushed by the falling tower and he  asked us do we think that we're better  than those people who died in the  accident is there some special reason we  feel like our time won't be cut short he  says don't get too comfortable we're no  more special than those people we have  no idea what the future holds and every  minute matters because every minute we  are at the mercy of the unpredictable  world do we want to spend our days in  fear and aimlessness or do we want to  spend them in faith and fruitfulness  there's a reason that the word deadline  contains the word dead the stories that  Paula Jesus tell put our daily walk into  the context of our ultimate end they are  about the ultimate finality of our lives  the highest stakes of our Christian  faith and the seriousness of our choices  they remind us that what we do matters a  lot more than we can fully know but  thankfully Paula Jesus don't end by  telling us about the deadline of death  they use it to get our attention but  then they give us a very different  message a message about life they throw  us a lifeline that we can cling to and  use to pull ourselves to safety  paul offers us a lifeline in the form of  a promise it's a mantra to take with us  as we head into midterms or writing  deadlines or the demands of  relationships or the workplace the  stresses of coping with illness or loss  or any other part of our struggle God is  faithful Paul tells us God is faithful  and God will not let you be tested  beyond your strength with the testing  God will also provide the way out so  that you may be able to endure it we may  be tested up to the limit we may reach  the end of our abilities but God is  faithful God is able and Jesus throws us  an equally sturdy lifeline a lifeline in  the form of a parable it's maybe my  favorite parable ever it's a story about  a fig tree this victory had gone for  three long years without burying any  fruit every year the owner of the tree  had walked to the vineyard looking for a  nice fresh fig and every year the tree  SAT paralyzed and unproductive maybe  making some leaves and flowers but  failing to actually do its job which was  to make figs and finally the owner of  the vineyard said to the gardener enough  already this victory is a waste of space  it's time to cut it down but the  gardener said just give me one more year  I'm going to try to help the tree out a  little I'm going to dig around it put  some fertilizer in the soil make sure it  has everything it needs and at the end  of the year if it bears fruit all as  well and if it doesn't go ahead and chop  it down  there are many things I love about this  story but the thing that I love most  about it is the gardener in most  interpretations of the story we are the  fig tree the gardener is Jesus and Jesus  is advocating for us we may have given  up hope we may have been looking around  the vineyard for the past three years  staring enviously at all the other trees  plastered with figs and we may have been  thinking that we would stay barren  forever and we may have been terrified  when he overheard the owner of the  vineyard talking saying it's time to cut  the tree down we may have agreed with  him we may have thought yep this  confirms what I always feared I'm a  waste of space go ahead and cut me down  but luckily it's not up to us the owner  does not get the last word and neither  do we and as we listen to the gardener  we learn that someone hasn't given up on  us someone thinks we might have figs in  our future and luckily for us the  someone's job is to take care of us his  job is to make sure we have air and  sunshine and fertilizer he wants to give  us another chance to fulfill our purpose  I also love that the gardener doesn't  let us off the hook he doesn't say you  know what figs are overrated who cares  about figs let's forget about fruit  let's just enjoy this tree for what it  is a bunch of bare branches the gardener  doesn't deny that figs are the trees  purpose and i love that the gardener  doesn't leave the timeline completely  open ended he doesn't say just give me  50 more years 50 years from now maybe  we'll see a fig know if it said that the  fig tree might have gone straight from  panic to procrastination and put off  making figs for another 49 years  the gardener knows that figs are  important time is of the essence so he  advocates for more time a limited amount  of time but enough time and then he gets  busy with the plowing and the  fertilizing and this is the hope that  the parable makes possible for us the  gardener's living care and the Sun and  the soil can help to awake in the deep  sense of purpose built into the fig  trees jeans instead of procrastination  and panic the tree will feel a deep  sustaining energy running in its trunk  singing in its SAP budding in its  branches with trust and care and just  the right amount of time figs can happen  Jesus doesn't tell us the end of the  story but that's okay because the story  of the parable is the story of our lives  we are living our lives in the shadow of  one very big deadline and many smaller  ones we may always be tempted to  procrastinate and panic but as Paul  reminds us we will never be tempted  beyond what we can bear and thanks to  the loving care of Jesus we have one  more day one more chance to bloom and  grow one more chance to turn our energy  into sweetness and sustenance one more  chance to turn faith into fruit sisters  and brothers it's not too late but it's  time let's go forth and make figs


